Why Is Demand Increasing?
Electric vehicle adoption: Expanding battery electric vehicle and plug-in hybrid production increases demand for automotive power semiconductor modules.
Higher-voltage architectures: The development of advanced vehicle electrical systems creates demand for power modules with appropriate voltage handling and efficiency characteristics.
Power electronics improvements: Advances in thermal management, module packaging and semiconductor design support performance improvements in electric drivetrains.
Electric Mobility & Automotive Power Electronics Watch
IGBT modules are critical components in new energy vehicle powertrains. Within traction inverters, they regulate the conversion of high-voltage direct current from vehicle batteries into alternating current for electric motors. Their switching performance, thermal characteristics and reliability can influence vehicle efficiency, power delivery and durability.
The expansion of electric mobility is increasing demand for automotive-grade power semiconductor components. Manufacturers are developing modules designed to withstand thermal cycling, vibration, humidity and other challenging automotive operating conditions. Improvements in packaging, cooling systems and electrical interconnections are also important as vehicle manufacturers seek greater power density and reliability.
The market faces challenges associated with semiconductor manufacturing costs, demanding automotive qualification processes, thermal management and competition among technology suppliers. The transition toward silicon-carbide power devices in selected applications also influences product development strategies. Buyers must therefore evaluate voltage requirements, switching performance, cooling design, reliability and total system cost when selecting power modules for next-generation vehicles.
